  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nIt's been a relatively cool and cloudy day across most of our\nforecast area, though there's been little rain to speak of so far.\nThere have been a few areas of slightly deeper cumulus across the\nnorthern mountains and along a weak surface boundary in Weld\ncounty which have produce some light showers. A few additional\nshowers could develop along any convergent boundary this afternoon\nand evening, but otherwise the rest of the afternoon should remain\nfairly quiet. \n\nAs we get into the evening hours, a very weak mid-level low is\nforecast to slide into northwestern Colorado, leading to a brief\nperiod of east-southeast upslope developing across the forecast\narea. Combined with some modest surface convergence and increasing\nMUCAPE, this should allow for a cluster of showers and \nthunderstorms to develop over the northern foothills this evening\ninto the early overnight hours. If the HREF/REFS members are to \nbe believed, some locally heavy rainfall would be possible before\nupslope quickly diminishes after midnight. Moist easterly surface\nflow overnight should allow for widespread fog and stratus to\ndevelop across the plains, with that wrapping into the\nnorthern/northwestern half of the Denver metro by early Friday.\n\nThe overall forecast changes very little tomorrow. The\nbroad/disorganized upper trough will kick out one last weak \nshortwave on Friday. We should see isolated to scattered\nshowers/thunderstorms develop by midday over the higher\nelevations, with this activity pushing eastward through the\nafternoon and evening. Deterministic guidance remains fairly\nbullish with metro precip chances, though I'd want to see how the\novernight fog/stratus plays out before committing to higher PoPs.\n\nThe exiting upper trough will quickly be replaced by weak ridging\nacross the region by Saturday, leading to a warm and dry weekend.\nMost statistical guidance warms temperatures into the mid 80s on\nboth Saturday and Sunday, with only enough moisture for some\nscattered cloud cover across the higher elevations on Sunday.\n\nAttention then turns to the potential for a stronger storm system\nto impact the region by sometime early/mid-week. Guidance\ngenerally agrees that a sharp upper trough axis will reach the\nnorthern California coastline by Sunday evening, which then drifts\ninto the desert southwest towards the Four Corners region by\nTuesday. Forecast confidence quickly decreases during this period,\nwith solutions ranging from a strong cutoff low over Baja\nCalifornia to a positively tilted trough that quickly swings into\nwestern Texas, with a few more favorable solutions scattered in\nbetween.\n\nThere are a few interesting tidbits across the past couple model\ncycles that are worth noting. First, while ensemble mean solutions\nlook favorable for precipitation here, there is a distinct bi-\nmodal distribution in forecast QPF within each respective ensemble\nsystem. This would imply that this storm is \"boom or bust\", with a\nfairly even split between boom/bust at this point in time. There\nare also additional complications from the interaction with what\nwould be the future remnants of Hurricane Polo. While the\ndeterministic forecast (NBM) is a fairly reasonable middle ground\n(i.e., cooler and increasing chances of rain/mountain snow), \npredictability and forecast confidence will be low for the next \nseveral days.",
